I have the full diff and guide docs. That is sufficient.

**What changed:** Angular 21.2 adds two new public `LocationStrategy` subclasses exported from `@angular/common` (declared in `packages/common/src/location/location_strategy.ts`, `@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})`, both extending `PathLocationStrategy` and overriding `prepareExternalUrl(internal: string)`): `TrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy`, which forces the path portion of every URL written to the browser to end with `/`, and `NoTrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy`, which strips a trailing `/` (except for the bare root `/`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8bbe6dc46c9dc13bafa81a60c7613b84b5ca3761). Query strings and fragments are preserved (`/a?q=val#frag` becomes `/a/?q=val#frag`). Only the URL *written* to history is affected — `Location.path()` and `Location.normalize()` still strip trailing slashes when reading, and no Router changes were made (matching still ignores trailing slashes). Addresses issue #16051. Documented in the new "Configure trailing slash handling" section of `adev/src/content/guide/routing/customizing-route-behavior.md`.

**Affects:** Opt-in only; existing apps are unchanged. Relevant to apps that need trailing slashes in the address bar (e.g. static-hosting/redirect rules) or that want to guarantee they never appear, and to code that customizes `LocationStrategy`/`PathLocationStrategy` or extends it.

**Action:** none for upgrading apps. To adopt: provide `{provide: LocationStrategy, useClass: TrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy}` (or `NoTrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy`) in application providers; re-verify generated `RouterLink` hrefs and server-side rewrite rules if the strategy is switched.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// new in 21.2 – force a trailing slash on URLs written to the browser
import {LocationStrategy, TrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy} from '@angular/common';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [{provide: LocationStrategy, useClass: TrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy}],
});

// or never a trailing slash
import {LocationStrategy, NoTrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy} from '@angular/common';

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [{provide: LocationStrategy, useClass: NoTrailingSlashPathLocationStrategy}],
});
```

**What changed:** `ImageLoaderConfig` in `@angular/common` gained an optional `height` property, which is passed to image loader functions alongside the existing `width`, `src`, `isPlaceholder`, and `loaderParams` fields (https://angular.dev/api/common/ImageLoaderConfig). This allows built-in loaders (such as `provideCloudflareLoader`, `provideCloudinaryLoader`, `provideImageKitLoader`, `provideImgixLoader`, and `provideNetlifyLoader`) and custom loader functions to generate URLs with explicit height parameters for better layout control and height-based transformations (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/51cc91480761b7275c15b5600381207f8ca00ee5).
**Affects:** Applications that define custom image loaders via `provideImageLoader` or use built-in image loaders when using `NgOptimizedImage`.
**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```typescript
interface ImageLoaderConfig {
  src: string;
  width: number;
  height?: number; // New property
  isPlaceholder: boolean;
  loaderParams?: {[key: string]: any};
}
```

**What changed:** A new opt-in feature for Angular's built-in control flow: `@switch` blocks can now enable exhaustive typechecking by adding `@default(never);` at the end of a `@switch` block. Declaring `never` explicitly asserts that no remaining cases should exist; if the switched value's union type is later extended and a new value is not covered by an `@case`, Angular's template type checker reports an error at compile time, catching missing branches early (commit 95b3f37d4a, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/95b3f37d4a7d9a38f616d56df746dfcda3c2139b; https://angular.dev/api/core/@switch). Per the API docs: exhaustiveness checking relies on TypeScript type narrowing, which only works on variables — it does not work if the switch condition is a function call or a signal call (e.g. `@switch (state())`); the workaround is to assign the signal to a `@let` variable, e.g. `@let mySignal = this.mySignal()`. When the switched expression is nested within a union, the expression to check for exhaustiveness must be specified explicitly (https://angular.dev/api/core/@switch).

**Affects:** Only templates that adopt the new syntax — existing `@switch` blocks behave exactly as before; no existing code breaks. Relevant for apps using `@switch` over union-typed values that want compile-time guarantees all cases are handled.

**Action:** none (opt-in feature). Optionally add `@default(never);` to `@switch` blocks over union types to get compile-time exhaustiveness errors; verify that the switch condition is a variable (not a signal call like `state()`) or introduce a `@let` alias.

**Before/after:**
```angular-html
@switch (mySignal) {
  @case ('caseA') { ... }
  @case ('caseB') { ... }
  @default(never) { }  <!-- error if the union gains a value with no @case -->
}
```
(Syntax based on `@default(never);` in the commit message and `@default` block semantics in https://angular.dev/api/core/@switch; exact snippet body not shown verbatim in fetched pages.)

**What changed:** In the Angular compiler-cli Type Check Block (TCB) generation, object literal keys in templates previously got TypeScript string-literal key nodes with no parse-span (`sourceSpan`) info attached, in `AstTranslator` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/expression.ts`. The fix calls `addParseSpanInfo(keyNode, key.sourceSpan)` on the key node before `ts.factory.createPropertyAssignment(...)`, so template diagnostics and Language Service positions for object literal keys now map back to the template. Concrete effect (from the updated specs): type errors inside an object literal assigned in a template event binding, e.g. `(click)="ref.tester = {t: 1, u: 0}"`, are now reported at earlier, more accurate columns in the `.html` file (e.g. `TestComponent.html(3, 61)` becomes `TestComponent.html(3, 58)` in `input_signal_diagnostics_spec.ts` / `model_signal_diagnostics_spec.ts`), and TCB span comments for `{{ m({foo: a, bar: b}) }}` now include spans on the keys (`"foo" /*6,9*/`) in `span_comments_spec.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/473dd3e1cbd4fe3fa88ae4d5358eee35c11acb1b.patch)

**Affects:** Applications whose templates bind or use object literals (e.g. `[input]="{key: value}"` or event handlers assigning object literals) and that rely on exact column numbers of template type-check diagnostics, or on Language Service completion/diagnostic positions for such keys.

**Action:** none — internal compiler-cli/language-service behaviour fix; no code, config, or dependency change required. Applications asserting on exact diagnostic line/column output from template type checking should re-verify expected locations.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/expression.ts
// before
return ts.factory.createPropertyAssignment(ts.factory.createStringLiteral(key.key), value);
// after
const keyNode = ts.factory.createStringLiteral(key.key);
addParseSpanInfo(keyNode, key.sourceSpan);
return ts.factory.createPropertyAssignment(keyNode, value);
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` Ivy transformation (`IvyTransformationVisitor.visitClassDeclaration` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/transform/src/transform.ts`) now resolves compilation results via `ts.getOriginalNode(node, ts.isClassDeclaration)` in addition to the visited node itself (`this.classCompilationMap.get(node) ?? this.classCompilationMap.get(original)`), so a class whose AST node was replaced by an earlier decorator transform is still transformed. Previously, with a nested component declaration such as an `@Component` class `Inner` declared inside the `constructor()` of an `@Component` class `Outer`, the Ivy transformation was only applied to `Inner`, which broke the transformation of `Outer` (the outer class lost its generated `ɵfac`/`ɵcmp` static fields). After the fix, both classes receive their `ɵɵngDeclareFactory` / `ɵɵngDeclareComponent` / `ɵɵngDeclareClassMetadata` output (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a904d9f77b56feab407f75f8d0527fa512d5dafb).

**Affects:** Applications that declare an Angular-decorated class (`@Component`, etc.) nested inside the body of another decorated class, e.g. inside a constructor — an unusual, likely invalid-in-practice pattern. No public API, property key, or dependency changed.

**Action:** none — this is a compiler bug fix inside `@angular/compiler-cli`; upgrading applications need no code, configuration, or dependency change. Applications that previously worked around the broken outer-class transform (e.g. by hoisting the nested class out) may keep or remove the workaround.

**Before/after:**
```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  template: 'outer',
})
class Outer {
  constructor() {
    @Component({
      template: 'inner',
    })
    class Inner {}
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The `DeferBlockData` interface returned by `getDeferBlocks` (the `@defer` debugging utility in `@angular/core`, source file `packages/core/src/render3/util/defer.ts`) gained a new field: `hostNode: Node` — "The comment host/container node next to which all of the root nodes are rendered." It is populated in `findDeferBlocks` from `details.lContainer[HOST] as Node`. This node serves as a `@defer` block locator in the DOM tree when `rootNodes` is empty (e.g., a `@defer` block with no declared `@placeholder`/`@loading`), and is used by Angular DevTools to render defer-only blocks in the component tree (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/92d2498910caed06c182b67e39726e1441418698, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/66546)

**Affects:** Applications or tooling (e.g. Angular DevTools extensions) that implement or consume the `DeferBlockData` shape from `getDeferBlocks`. Framework-only addition; no existing application code breaks.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// DeferBlockData in packages/core/src/render3/util/defer.ts (21.2.0)
  /** Stringified version of the block's triggers. */
  triggers: string[];

+ /** The comment host/container node next to which all of the root nodes are rendered. */
+ hostNode: Node;

  /** Element root nodes that are currently being shown in the block. */
  rootNodes: Node[];
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.2.0 adds support for nested `animate.leave` animations (commit `ea2016a6dc`, fixes #66476). Previously, only a leave animation registered on the exact node being removed ran before DOM removal; leave animations declared on descendants inside a template (in the same view, in `@if` embedded views, or in child component views/host bindings) were dropped because the parent element was removed immediately. Now, when a node is removed, Angular collects leave animations from descendants of that node (`aggregateDescendantAnimations` over the view's `leave` animation map), from nested embedded views/containers and child component views (`collectNestedViewAnimations` / `collectAllViewLeaveAnimations` in the new `packages/core/src/render3/node_animations.ts`), runs them all, and only removes the parent after the returned promises settle (`runLeaveAnimationsWithCallback`, `executeLeaveAnimations`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ea2016a6dce58f95ecab7c773d5dcde274354e1a, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/66476). Removal is therefore deferred until every nested `animate.leave` completes, including ones declared via `host: {'animate.leave': ...}`, `host: {'(animate.leave)': ...}`, `(animate.leave)="fn($event)"` with `event.animationComplete()`, or signal-input-driven host bindings. The `ANIMATION_QUEUE` factory in `packages/core/src/animation/queue.ts` now registers `injector.onDestroy(() => queue.clear())`, and a guard avoids "Injector destroyed" errors when a tree with nested leave animations is destroyed.

**Affects:** Applications with CSS-class or callback `animate.leave` (or `(animate.leave)` / host `animate.leave`) attached to elements or component hosts nested inside an element that is itself removed (e.g. inside `@if`, `@for`, or a parent component being torn down), or apps relying on such elements disappearing synchronously with the parent.

**Action:** none — this is an additive capability; no code, dependency, or configuration change is required. Re-verify that removal-dependent logic (timers, cleanup, tests asserting `querySelector(...) === null` right after `detectChanges()`) still behaves correctly now that the parent node persists until nested leave animations finish.

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- Before: parent removed immediately, child's leave animation never ran.
     From 21.2: the .child leave animation runs, then the parent is removed. -->
@if (show()) {
  <div class="parent">
    <div animate.leave="fade" class="child">Child</div>
  </div>
}

<!-- Also supported nested now: component host binding -->
@Component({
  selector: 'fade-cmp',
  host: {'animate.leave': 'fade'},
  template: '<p>I should fade</p>',
})
class FadeComponent {}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.2.0 updates its supported TypeScript range from `5.9.x` to `5.9.0–6.0.x` (with `6.1.0` as the upper exclusive bound). In `@angular/compiler-cli`'s `packages/compiler-cli/src/typescript_support.ts`, `MAX_TS_VERSION` was changed from `"6.0.0"` to `"6.1.0"`, and `MIN_TS_VERSION` was changed from `"5.9.0"` to `"5.9.0"` (remains unchanged) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/81cabc14777a3b4966c29d60e1505aca8c29b71c). The `package.json` `peerDependencies` entry for `typescript` changed from `>=5.9 <6.0` to `>=5.9 <6.1`. The project itself (root `package.json`) switched from `"typescript": "5.9.3"` to `"typescript": "6.0.0-beta"`.

**Affects:** applications using `@angular/compiler-cli` 21.2.0 that pin or lock `typescript` at a version outside the supported range.

**Action:** required: update the `typescript` dependency in your `package.json` (or lockfile) to a version that satisfies `>=5.9 <6.1`; `6.0.0-beta` is the version the Angular repo resolved for this release. If TypeScript `6.x` has not yet been released, the supported range effectively remains `5.9.x` (no change required). Re-verify type-checking and build after any version change.

**Before/after:**
```json
// @angular/compiler-cli peerDependencies
// Before:
"typescript": ">=5.9 <6.0"
// After:
"typescript": ">=5.9 <6.1"
```
```json
// root package.json dependencies
// Before:
"typescript": "5.9.3"
// After:
"typescript": "6.0.0-beta"
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.2.0 adds resource composition primitives in `@angular/core`. A new type `ResourceSnapshot<T>` is defined as a union of the possible states of a `Resource<T>`: `{status: 'idle'; value: T} | {status: 'loading' | 'reloading'; value: T} | {status: 'resolved' | 'local'; value: T} | {status: 'error'; error: Error}`, and is marked `@experimental`. The `Resource<T>` interface gains a new member `readonly snapshot: Signal<ResourceSnapshot<T>>`, which converts a `Resource` into a signal of its snapshot. A new function `resourceFromSnapshots<T>(source: () => ResourceSnapshot<T>): Resource<T>` converts a reactive snapshot (a signal, or a function wrapped in `computed`) back into a `Resource`; its `value` throws a `ResourceValueError` when the snapshot status is `'error'`. Together these allow full resource composition on top of signal APIs like `computed` and `linkedSignal`, e.g. a `Resource` that retains its previous value while its params change (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1ba9b7ac5001b315cc9df78c518964dbf479d647).

**Affects:** No existing code is affected; purely additive API. Relevant only to applications using `resource()` / `httpResource()` that want resource-to-resource composition, or third-party code implementing the `Resource<T>` interface manually (which must now provide a `snapshot` member).

**Action:** none for upgrading applications. Optionally, adopt the new API to build resource utilities: import `resourceFromSnapshots` and use `resource(...).snapshot` with `linkedSignal`, as shown below. Note the API is labelled `@experimental`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New in 21.2: retain the previous value while a resource reloads
function withPreviousValue<T>(input: Resource<T>): Resource<T> {
  const derived = linkedSignal({
    source: input.snapshot,
    computation: (snap, previous) => {
      if (snap.status === 'loading' && previous?.value) {
        return {status: 'loading', value: previous.value.value};
      }
      return snap;
    },
  });

  return resourceFromSnapshots(derived);
}

userId = input.required<number>();
user = withPreviousValue(httpResource(() => `/user/{this.userId()}`));
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.2.0, the core DI function `lookupTokenUsingEmbeddedInjector` (in `packages/core/src/render3/di.ts`) no longer passes the `SkipSelf` flag when delegating a lookup to an embedded view injector. Previously it called `(embeddedViewInjector as BackwardsCompatibleInjector).get(token, NOT_FOUND, flags)`; now it calls it with `flags & ~InternalInjectFlags.SkipSelf` (commit a7e8abbb7e, message: "The `SkipSelf` flag is intended for the current injection context. When delegating to an embedded view injector, we are traversing to a parent/fallback scope, so the 'Self' has already been skipped."). This is a behavioral bug fix (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a7e8abbb7e738ba338c3f50c76934c99925954e5).

**Affects:** Applications that resolve a token from within an embedded view (e.g. inside an `*ngIf`/`@if` template or via `TemplateRef`-created views) using `@SkipSelf()` or `inject(TOKEN, {skipSelf: true})`, where the token would have been provided by the embedded view injector itself. Under 21.1 such lookups could incorrectly fail to resolve from the embedded view injector; in 21.2 the embedded view injector is now allowed to resolve the token from itself.

**Action:** none — no code, config, or dependency change required; only re-verify that DI lookups using `SkipSelf` inside embedded views still resolve the expected instance (an app that relied on the old buggy non-resolution behavior could now find a different provider).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (21.1): SkipSelf propagated into the embedded view injector
const embeddedViewInjectorValue = (embeddedViewInjector as BackwardsCompatibleInjector).get(
  token,
  NOT_FOUND as T | {},
  flags,
);

// After (21.2): SkipSelf stripped before delegating
const embeddedViewInjectorValue = (embeddedViewInjector as BackwardsCompatibleInjector).get(
  token,
  NOT_FOUND as T | {},
  flags & ~InternalInjectFlags.SkipSelf,
);
```
(Both snippets from https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a7e8abbb7e738ba338c3f50c76934c99925954e5)

**What changed:** An internal core animation fix (commit 0806ee3826, "fix(core): prevent animated element duplication with dynamic components in zoneless mode") changed how Angular cancels in-flight leave animations when the same animated node is re-inserted. Previously `cancelLeavingNodes(tNode, lView)` ran during template execution inside `ɵɵanimateEnter`, `ɵɵanimateEnterListener`, `ɵɵanimateLeave` and `ɵɵanimateLeaveListener`, and located the leaving element via the declaration container anchor (`DECLARATION_LCONTAINER` / `getBeforeNodeForView`) — which fails for overlay/portal patterns (e.g. CDK Overlay) where root nodes are moved to a separate container, so rapid `ViewContainerRef` toggling left queued leave animations unexecuted and multiple copies appeared in the DOM (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0806ee38269b664f535e10d4d501b88370d3b44c). Now `cancelLeavingNodes(tNode, newElement)` is called from `node_manipulation.ts` on `WalkTNodeTreeAction.Insert` (after `nativeInsertBefore`), and `trackLeavingNodes` is called on `Detach` when the tNode has leave animations; it cancels (dispatching `CustomEvent('animationend', {detail: {cancel: true}})`) and removes leaving elements that are either the new element's `previousSibling` or in a different DOM parent, while leaving same-parent non-adjacent leaving nodes (e.g. `@for` items) alone. The internal helper `removeAnimationsFromQueue` was deleted from `packages/core/src/animation/queue.ts` and is no longer called from `runLeaveAnimationsWithCallback`.

**Affects:** Zoneless (`provideZonelessChangeDetection`-style) applications that animate elements created dynamically via `ViewContainerRef`/`createEmbeddedView` and move them into another container (CDK Overlay, `DomPortalOutlet`, portal patterns), or that rapidly toggle animated `@if` blocks; only the observable duplicate-element bug is fixed — the API is unchanged (`removeAnimationsFromQueue`, `cancelLeavingNodes`, `trackLeavingNodes`, `leavingNodes` appear only as internal/mangled names in `bundle.golden_symbols.json`, not public exports).

**Action:** none — no application code, template, or configuration change is required; applications relying on the old duplicated-element appearance (e.g. tests asserting several copies of an animated element in the DOM) should re-verify such DOM assertions.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (21.1): cancelLeavingNodes ran in template instructions and used the declaration container anchor
export function cancelLeavingNodes(tNode: TNode, lView: LView): void {
  const leavingEl = leavingNodes.get(tNode)?.shift();
  const lContainer = lView[DECLARATION_LCONTAINER];
  if (lContainer) {
    const beforeNode = getBeforeNodeForView(tNode.index, lContainer);
    const previousNode = beforeNode?.previousSibling;
    if (leavingEl && previousNode && leavingEl === previousNode) {
      leavingEl.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('animationend', {detail: {cancel: true}}));
    }
  }
}

// after (21.2): called from node_manipulation.ts after nativeInsertBefore (Insert) /
// trackLeavingNodes on Detach; cancels by previousSibling or different DOM parent
export function cancelLeavingNodes(tNode: TNode, newElement: HTMLElement): void {
  const nodes = leavingNodes.get(tNode);
  if (!nodes || nodes.length === 0) return;
  const newParent = newElement.parentNode;
  const prevSibling = newElement.previousSibling;
  for (let i = nodes.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    const leavingEl = nodes[i];
    const leavingParent = leavingEl.parentNode;
    if (
      (prevSibling && leavingEl === prevSibling) ||
      (leavingParent && newParent && leavingParent !== newParent)
    ) {
      nodes.splice(i, 1);
      leavingEl.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('animationend', {detail: {cancel: true}}));
      leavingEl.parentNode?.removeChild(leavingEl);
    }
  }
}
```

**What changed:** In the signal forms API (`@angular/forms/signals`), `FormSubmitOptions` gained a second generic and its callbacks gained a second parameter. Old: `FormSubmitOptions<TModel>` with `action: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => Promise<TreeValidationResult>` and `onInvalid?: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => void`. New: `FormSubmitOptions<TRootModel, TSubmittedModel>` with `action: (field: FieldTree<TRootModel & TSubmittedModel>, detail: {root: FieldTree<TRootModel>; submitted: FieldTree<TSubmittedModel>}) => Promise<TreeValidationResult>` and the same second `detail` argument on `onInvalid`. `field` is the root field when options come from `FormOptions.submission` at `form()` creation, and the submitted field when options are passed to `submit()`. `FormOptions<TModel>['submission']` is now typed `FormSubmitOptions<TModel, unknown>`, and the `submit()` overloads take `NoInfer<FormSubmitOptions<unknown, TModel>>`. The interface moved from `api/structure.ts` to `api/types.ts`, and a `submit()` method on field state was removed in favor of the standalone importable `submit()` function (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f56bb07d83a015b0ac12e74fdb0cf1550ff36b97).

**Affects:** Applications using the experimental signal forms submission API — i.e. declaring `submission: { action, onInvalid }` in `form()` options, passing options/action to `submit()`, or importing `FormSubmitOptions` — and any code calling a `submit()` method on a `FieldState`.

**Action:** required: update `action`/`onInvalid` callbacks to the two-argument signature; where code relied on the first argument being the submitted tree (options given to `submit()`), use `detail.submitted`, and for the whole form use `detail.root`. Add the second generic when annotating `FormSubmitOptions<...>`. Replace any `fieldState.submit()` usage with the standalone `submit(fieldTree, ...)`. Re-verify submission error reporting (`TreeValidationResult` field trees) since `field` semantics now depend on where options were declared.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (21.1)
export interface FormSubmitOptions<TModel> {
  action: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => Promise<TreeValidationResult>;
  onInvalid?: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => void;
}
await submit(f.first, { action: (form) => { submitSpy(form().value()); return Promise.resolve(undefined); } });

// after (21.2)
export interface FormSubmitOptions<TRootModel, TSubmittedModel> {
  action: (
    field: FieldTree<TRootModel & TSubmittedModel>,
    detail: {root: FieldTree<TRootModel>; submitted: FieldTree<TSubmittedModel>},
  ) => Promise<TreeValidationResult>;
  onInvalid?: (
    field: FieldTree<TRootModel & TSubmittedModel>,
    detail: {root: FieldTree<TRootModel>; submitted: FieldTree<TSubmittedModel>},
  ) => void;
}
await submit(f.first, { action: (_, {root, submitted}) => { submitSpy(root().value(), submitted().value()); return Promise.resolve(undefined); } });
```

I have the full patch, including the new `FormRoot` directive source, its public API golden entry, and the updated Angular docs (`field-state-management.md`) showing before/after usage. That is sufficient.

**What changed:** Angular 21.2.0 adds a new `FormRoot` directive (exported from `@angular/forms/signals`, file `packages/forms/signals/src/directive/ng_signal_form.ts`, marked `@experimental 21.0.0`) with selector `form[formRoot]` and a required signal input `fieldTree` aliased as `formRoot` (`input.required<FieldTree<T>>({alias: 'formRoot'})`). It sets `novalidate` on the `<form>` element via host bindings and listens for `(submit)`, calling `event.preventDefault()` then `submit(this.fieldTree())` on the `FieldTree` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ba009b603119299a03f9d844f93882d42d47d150). The docs previously told developers to write `<form novalidate (submit)="onSubmit($event)">` and call `submit(this.registrationForm, async () => this.submitToServer())` manually; they now show `<form [formRoot]="registrationForm">` with the submission callback declared in `form()` options as `submission: {action: async () => ...}`, and note `submit()` can still be called manually with `FormSubmitOptions` (`submit(this.registrationForm, {action: () => ...})`). The docs also show `reset()` now accepting an optional value: `f().reset({...this.INITIAL_MODEL})` (same URL). It works alongside `FormsModule` and `ReactiveFormsModule` imports (tested in `ng_signal_form.spec.ts`).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`, `form()`, `FieldTree`) that handle `<form>` submission; opt-in only — no existing behavior changes.

**Action:** none (optional: import `FormRoot` from `@angular/forms/signals` in standalone components and replace manual `novalidate` + `(submit)`/`$event.preventDefault()`/`submit(...)` handlers with `<form [formRoot]="myForm">` plus a `submission: {action: ...}` option in `form()`).

**Before/after:**
```angular-ts
// Before
<form novalidate (submit)="onSubmit($event)">
// onSubmit: event.preventDefault(); submit(this.registrationForm, async () => this.submitToServer());

// After
@Component({
  imports: [FormRoot, FormField],
  template: `
    <form [formRoot]="registrationForm">
      <input [formField]="registrationForm.username" />
    </form>
  `,
})
export class Registration {
  registrationForm = form(this.registrationModel, (schemaPath) => {/* ... */}, {
    submission: {action: async () => this.submitToServer()},
  });
}
```

**What changed:** Native form inputs (e.g. `<input type=number>`) now integrate with the new `parseErrors` API. When a user types an un-parsable value such as `"42e"` in a number field, the model does not update — it retains its previous value and a parse error is added for the control that received the bad value (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/22afbb2f36be89c2ae575df343571a918dec5985). The `ControlValueAccessor` interface now includes an optional readonly `parseErrors` signal; Signal Forms (`FormField`) reads this signal if present and propagates it to the field state (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/67900). Binding `null` to a number input is now supported: binding `null` or `NaN` clears the input, and clearing the input sets the model to `null`; the model is never set to `NaN` based on user interaction (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/30f09147545b67185f93efb9796e37c1db76733a). `parsedErrors` is implemented as a `linkedSignal` based on the model value, so parse errors reset if the model changes from outside the control (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/27397b3f4f3182ce00d6e2f8690285c316e2a274).

**Affects:** Applications using native `<input type=number>` bound via `[(ngModel)]`, `formControl`, `formControlName`, or Signal Forms `FormField`, especially those that previously relied on the model being set to `NaN`/unchanged when an invalid number was entered, or that read number-input values after clearing the field.

**Action:** required: re-verify number-input handling. Custom `ControlValueAccessor` implementations may optionally expose a readonly `parseErrors` signal to surface parse errors to Signal Forms. Applications that treated cleared number inputs as `NaN` must be re-checked, since clearing now yields `null`. Verify number fields no longer produce `NaN` and that parse errors propagate via `FormField` state.

