**What changed:** In `@angular/forms/signals`, the `submit` function signature changed from `submit<TModel>(form: FieldTree<TModel>, action: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => Promise<TreeValidationResult>): Promise<void>` to `submit<TModel>(form: FieldTree<TModel>, options: FormSubmitOptions<TModel>): Promise<boolean>`. The new `FormSubmitOptions<TModel>` interface (marked `@experimental 21.2.0`) has `action: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => Promise<TreeValidationResult>`, optional `onInvalid?: (form: FieldTree<TModel>) => void`, and optional `ignoreValidators?: 'pending' | 'none' | 'all'` (default `'pending'`: pending validators do not block submission; `'none'` requires all validators passing; `'all'` always submits). The return value is now a `Promise<boolean>` indicating submission success. Also, `adapter?: FieldAdapter` was removed from the public `FormOptions` (now `@internal`), so `FormOptions` publicly exposes only `injector?: Injector` and `name?: string` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/dd208ca2595258fcd1e289374f812ce0b56c7011)

**Affects:** Applications calling `submit(...)` from `@angular/forms/signals`, or passing `adapter` in `FormOptions` to `form()`.

**Action:** required: Wrap the existing submit callback in an options object under the `action` property, i.e. `submit(f, {action: async (form) => {...}})`; optionally add `onInvalid` and `ignoreValidators`. Where code awaited `submit(...)` for its void result, re-verify logic using the new `Promise<boolean>` return. Remove any `adapter` option passed to `form()`'s `FormOptions` (now internal).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
submit(registrationForm, async (f) => {
  return registerNewUser(registrationForm);
});
// after
submit(registrationForm, {
  action: async (f) => {
    return registerNewUser(registrationForm);
  }
});
```

**What changed:** In signal forms, `FormField` (`packages/forms/signals/src/directive/form_field_directive.ts`) previously stored a custom control's focus callback directly: `this.focuser = bindingOptions.focus;`. Because the method was detached from its object, calling `field().focusBoundControl()` invoked a custom control's `focus()` method with a lost `this` context, so any code reading instance members (e.g. a `viewChild.required<ElementRef<HTMLInputElement>>('input')` signal) threw `TypeError: this.input is not a function` at runtime (). The fix in 21.2.0 wraps the callback so invocation keeps the original context: `this.focuser = (focusOptions?: FocusOptions) => bindingOptions.focus!(focusOptions);` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/63d80057039928b3e878b59c1fe6b93ef1c6b701).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`) that bind a custom control via `@Connection`/`field(...)` options providing a `focus` callback (i.e. a control class defining a `focus()` method that uses `this`), and call `focusBoundControl()`.

**Action:** none — the fix is internal to Angular; previously broken `focusBoundControl()` calls on custom controls with instance-member-reading `focus()` methods now work. Re-verify that custom `focus()` methods receive `FocusOptions` and focus the intended inner element.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (this lost when focusBoundControl() called focus())
this.focuser = bindingOptions.focus;
// after
this.focuser = (focusOptions?: FocusOptions) => bindingOptions.focus!(focusOptions);
```

**What changed:** A bug fix in Signal Forms: previously, when a custom `FormValueControl` used `transformedValue` and its `parse()` returned both a `value` and `errors` (e.g. `{value, errors: [maxError(10)]}`), the errors were discarded — the field value updated but `field.errors()` came back empty (regression introduced in rc.0, worked in next.3) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/67170). In `packages/forms/signals/src/util/parser.ts`, the internal `createParser`'s `setRawValue` now calls `errors.set(result.errors ?? [])` **after** `setValue(result.value)`, because `errors` is a linked signal sourced from the model value and writing it before the model update caused it to be reset during recomputation (). Now both value and parse errors from a `{value, errors}` result are preserved (e.g. entering `11` for a max-10 parse yields `kind: 'max'` in `f().errors()`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/631f60d1f9be72cb68330308a6ff18cc195babb8).

**Affects:** Applications using Signal Forms `transformedValue` with a `parse()` function that returns both `value` and `errors` — these now see the previously-swallowed errors surface in `field.errors()`, which can change validation/UI state.

**Action:** none — no code change required; re-verify UI/validation behaviour for controls whose `parse()` returns `{value, errors}`, since errors that were silently dropped in 21.1/rc.0 now appear.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/forms/signals/src/util/parser.ts — setRawValue
// before
const result = parse(rawValue);
errors.set(result.errors ?? []);
if (result.value !== undefined) {
  setValue(result.value);
}

// after
const result = parse(rawValue);
if (result.value !== undefined) {
  setValue(result.value);
}
// `errors` is a linked signal sourced from the model value; write parse errors after
// model updates so `{value, errors}` results do not get reset by the recomputation.
errors.set(result.errors ?? []);
```

**What changed:** In signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`), the optional `parseErrors` signal property was removed from both the `FormUiControl<TValue>` interface and `FormFieldBindingOptions` (used by `FormField.registerAsBinding`). It is replaced by a new experimental (21.2.0) utility `transformedValue<TValue, TRaw>(value: ModelSignal<TValue>, options: TransformedValueOptions<TValue, TRaw>): TransformedValueSignal<TRaw>`, exported from `@angular/forms/signals`. `TransformedValueOptions` has `parse: (rawValue: TRaw) => {value?: TValue; errors?: readonly ValidationError.WithoutFieldTree[]}` and `format: (value: TValue) => TRaw`. The returned `TransformedValueSignal<TRaw>` extends `WritableSignal<TRaw>` and exposes `parseErrors: Signal<readonly ValidationError.WithoutFieldTree[]>`; calling `.set`/`.update` runs `parse`, updates the model only when `value !== undefined`, and reports errors to the enclosing `FormField` automatically via a new injection token `FORM_FIELD_PARSE_ERRORS` (also experimental) provided by the `FormField` directive (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/adfb83146b0c149734f43961563b389e00cc1d85).

**Affects:** Custom controls implementing `FormValueControl`/`FormUiControl` in signal forms that declared a `parseErrors` signal or passed `parseErrors` to `FormField.registerAsBinding(...)` / `FormFieldBindingOptions`.

**Action:** required: remove the `parseErrors` member from classes implementing `FormUiControl` and remove `parseErrors` from objects passed to `FormField.registerAsBinding`; instead create the raw control value with `transformedValue(this.value, {parse, format})`, bind the UI to its `.set()`/value, and read errors via `rawValue.parseErrors` — reporting to `FormField` is now automatic.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (removed in 21.2.0)
class CustomControl implements FormValueControl<string> {
  readonly value = model.required<string>();
  readonly parseErrors = computed(() => (this.value() === 'ERROR' ? [{kind: 'parse'}] : []));
}

// After (from the transformedValue JSDoc example)
export class NumberInput implements FormValueControl<number | null> {
  value = model.required<number | null>();
  protected readonly rawValue = transformedValue(this.value, {
    parse: (val) => {
      if (val === '') return {value: null};
      const num = Number(val);
      if (Number.isNaN(num)) {
        return {errors: [{kind: 'parse', message: `${val} is not numeric`}]};
      }
      return {value: num};
    },
    format: (val) => val?.toString() ?? '',
  });
}
```

**What changed:** The `errorSummary()` signal on field trees in the signal-based forms API (`@angular/forms/signals`) now returns validation and parse errors sorted by DOM order, instead of tree/registration order. Previously `errorSummary` was computed via `reduceChildren` over the field tree structure; now, in `packages/forms/signals/src/field/validation.ts`, `FieldValidationState.errorSummary` sorts errors with a new `compareErrorPosition` helper using `compareDocumentPosition` on the element bound by `FormField` (`error.formField.element` or the earliest of `fieldTree().formFieldBindings()`). Rules: for an error bound to multiple elements, the earliest DOM element is used; errors with no field binding sort after all bound errors. Sorting runs only on the client — it is skipped when `ngServerMode` is defined, so on the server the old (unsorted) order remains. Per the commit message, "This will allow users to rely on the `errorSummary` order to implement features like 'focus next error'" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fb05fc86d0f12ffafd94c7c1420118d8a79f7e59).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()`, `FormField` from `@angular/forms/signals`) that read `errorSummary()` and depend on its ordering (e.g. error lists, "focus next error"); ordering may differ from 21.1 and may differ between SSR-rendered and client output.

**Action:** required: re-verify any UI logic consuming `errorSummary()` — it now appears in DOM order (unbound errors last); tests asserting the old tree order need updating. Code must bind fields with `FormField` for DOM sorting to apply.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before: errorSummary followed field-tree structure order
readonly errorSummary = computed(() =>
  this.node.structure.reduceChildren(this.errors(), (child, result) => [
    ...result,
    ...child.errorSummary(),
  ]),
);

// after (client only): sorted by DOM position of the bound form field element
if (typeof ngServerMode === 'undefined' || !ngServerMode) {
  untracked(() => errors.sort(compareErrorPosition));
}
```

